;; bedit — a super-minimal GUI text editor, built on the rope-backed buffer
;; framework (editor/buffer) + the ui-run loop (editor/ui). The M2 editor app:
;; a standalone `nest` project that `(:use editor/buffer)`s the bundled editor
;; framework. See src/main.blsp.
;;
;; Run it (needs a GUI-enabled `nest` — `./configure --with-gui && make install`
;; in the brood repo):
;;     nest run                 ; a scratch buffer
;;     nest run -- notes.txt    ; open (Ctrl-S saves) that file
;;
;; The editor depends only on bundled std modules — `net/*` (web mirror) and
;; `supervisor` (the logger supervisor) ship in the default `nest` install
;; (ADR-097, batteries-included), so there are no external source-paths.
(project
  :name "bedit"
  :version "0.8.1"
  ;; The floor is what bedit actually needs, not the version it was first written on:
  ;; `os/spawn-pty` (the project REPL under a pty, src/bshell.blsp) and `sort`'s
  ;; comparator-last order both arrived in 0.26.0, and 0.27.1 is the first release whose
  ;; checker narrows `filter` on a type predicate — which is what makes `nest check` (the
  ;; CI gate) clean on the failure-value idiom this code is written in.
  ;;
  ;; 0.28.0 is the release the lexical modes ride: `regex/tokens` / `regex/paint` (ADR-352),
  ;; `editor/highlight/line-restart`, the one-scan `editor/lexer` and `editor/shell` — an
  ;; older `nest` opens a shell buffer to `unbound symbol` on every frame; and `humanize/*`,
  ;; which the byte-size columns call.
  ;;
  ;; 0.30.0 is where `fuzzy/top` lives — the completion seam ranks through it, so an older
  ;; runtime does not merely rank slower, it raises `unbound symbol: fuzzy/top` at the first
  ;; keystroke in a find-file prompt. It also carries the sRGB text blend the default
  ;; `:text-contrast` of 1.0 assumes, and the checker the strict ratchet is ceilinged
  ;; against (a `let`-bound lambda's parameters derived from its callers, `apply` binding a
  ;; callee's type variable, and the node budget that stopped flattening `model`).
  ;;
  ;; 0.4.9's per-pane zoom paints a `cell-region` and asks `gui/cell-size` (brood ADR-363),
  ;; the LSP asks `os/which` before spawning a server (a missing `elixir-ls` is a message,
  ;; not a crash), and the git journal spells an elapsed time with `humanize/duration`.
  ;; All three are in 0.32.0; on an older runtime each raises `unbound symbol` at the
  ;; moment it is reached rather than at load. The floor tracks the release bedit is tested
  ;; against: 0.40.0. On 0.39.0 a JIT-compiled function ignored its `&optional` default
  ;; (brood KI-270), and bedit leans on `&optional` defaults in over a hundred places.
  ;;
  ;; 0.40.0 is the first release with `std/registry` and `reflect/call-if-bound` (brood
  ;; ADR-412) — every table a feature fills at load is one, so a 0.39.1 runtime raises
  ;; `unbound symbol: registry/put!` at the first module that registers anything — and with
  ;; `editor/buffer-registry`'s `{:mirror true}` (collab's daemon), `editor/list-motion`,
  ;; `editor/transient` short rows, `editor/org` (org-mode), `editor/treesit/languages` and
  ;; grammar subdirectories (M-x grammar-install), guest links (ADR-415, a shared session's
  ;; invites) and the KI-277 fix, without which `nest run`'s contracts made every pty
  ;; driver time out.
  ;;
  ;; 0.42.0 removed std's editor features (ADR-417); bedit carries them itself (org, section,
  ;; transient-core, formbuf, lex-*, mail-message), so it no longer needs them from std. The
  ;; floor moves with the ecosystem: every broodlang repo is verified against the same release.
  ;;
  ;; 0.43.0: the TLS trust fixes every `https` fetch takes (RUSTSEC-2026-0381/-0382, 0.42.2)
  ;; and module loads that park instead of blocking a worker (KI-283/284/285) — the editor
  ;; loads its deferred modules from many processes at once. A `~name/` in a file prompt
  ;; needs the release after it (`path/absolute` over `%user-home`); on 0.43.0 it reads as
  ;; a relative name, and nothing else changes.
  ;;
  ;; 0.44.0: std's `vcs` (git or mur: the project root, its files, the change gutter's base
  ;; text, the mode line), `os/shell-split` (`!` in the git and mur status buffers),
  ;; `path/substitute` (every file prompt's `$VAR`, `//` and `/~/`), `std/settings` (every
  ;; config key) and tree-sitter tree handles (the kept parse each buffer holds). On 0.43.0
  ;; each one raises `unbound symbol` where it is reached.
  ;;
  ;; 0.45.0: a tree handle owned by its process and handed over with `give-tree` (the parse
  ;; a task warms comes back to the loop, brood ADR-495), `diff/map-line` / `hunk-changes` /
  ;; `hunks-status` (the time machine, and point kept on its line across a revert, ADR-499),
  ;; fuzzy ranking with `:category :file`, operators and a boost (find-file and every
  ;; completion, ADR-497), injections with `:exclude` (a Dockerfile heredoc, ADR-496), C-g
  ;; through a busy update (ADR-491/493/494/502) and vt rows of several colours painted in
  ;; order. On 0.44.0 the first four raise `unbound symbol` or reject an option where they
  ;; are reached.
  ;;
  ;; 0.47.0: the bare namespace is the language (brood ADR-506) — `seq/sort-by`, `map/get-in`,
  ;; `combinator/juxt`, `proc/sleep`, `reflect/doc`, `editor/ui/*term-display*` and the rest
  ;; of the 113 moved names. On 0.46.0 every one of them is `unbound symbol` where it is reached.
  ;;
  ;; 0.48.0: a tagged `buffer-edit` takes its origin before the transform (brood ADR-507),
  ;; `(buffer-edit proc me (fn …))`. On 0.47.0 the transform lands in the origin slot and the
  ;; pid is called as the transform.
  :brood ">= 0.49.0"
  :kind :app ; an application you run, not a library you depend on (hive listing)
  :description
    "An Emacs-style editor written in Brood: rope-backed buffers, rebindable keymaps, modes, LSP, git, and an eval sandbox — pure Brood glue over the std/editor toolkit and the ui-run loop."
  :repository "https://github.com/broodlang/bedit"
  ;; No build dependencies: bedit is pure glue over bundled std. Theme (and other) plugins
  ;; are installed at RUNTIME into ~/.config/bedit/packages via M-x package-list — not here.
  :source-paths ["src"])
